%% posix-on-wasm.mgp - a gucOS talk (todos/0221, rewritten 2026-07-17).
%% What remains of an operating system when WebAssembly runs in a browser?
%deffont "standard" tfont "/usr/share/fonts/mono.ttf"
%default 1 area 90 90, size 2, fore "white", back "#301848", font "standard"
%default 2 size 8, vgap 20, prefix " ", center
%default 3 size 5, fore "gold", vgap 30
%tab 1 size 4, vgap 35, prefix "  ", icon box "SpringGreen" 45, left
%tab 2 size 3, vgap 35, prefix "      ", icon arc "gold" 45, left
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

POSIX on WebAssembly

%size 5, fore "gold", center
or: what is an operating system anyway?
%size 3, fore "gray70", center

a tour through gucOS
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

The familiar picture

%size 5, center, fore "white"
applications
%bar "gray70" 10 30 40
operating system
%bar "gray70" 10 30 40
hardware
%size 4, fore "gray70", center

The OS talks to the machine on our behalf.
True - but radically incomplete.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

An OS is a bundle of promises

	start programs and keep them apart
	give them files, terminals, sound and windows
	let them communicate - and survive failure
	turn one machine into a shared environment
%size 4, center, fore "gold"

Hardware control is one job in that bundle.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

POSIX names the promises

	files and file descriptors
	processes, signals, pipes and terminals
	a C interface programs can be written against
%size 4, fore "gray70", center

POSIX is not a kernel, a desktop, or hardware.
It is a contract between programs and a system.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

"Isn't Emscripten already POSIX?"

%size 6, fore "SpringGreen", center
Yes: it helps one Unix-shaped program run.
%pause
%size 6, fore "orange", center
No: it does not hand you a whole OS.
%size 4, fore "gray70", center

Process ownership, a shared desktop, persistent
state and system-wide services are still real work.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

The browser changes the question

%size 5, center
Not:
%size 6, fore "orange"
"How do we copy Unix into WebAssembly?"
%size 5, fore "white"

Instead:
%size 6, fore "SpringGreen"
"Which OS jobs still belong to us?"
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

Triage the job description

%size 7, fore "SpringGreen", center
DO
%pause
%size 7, fore "orange"
DON'T
%pause
%size 7, fore "DeepSkyBlue"
DON'T NEED TO
%size 4, fore "gray70"

Three buckets. Three different reasons.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

DO: make processes real

	each program is its own WebAssembly instance
	each gets isolated linear memory and a worker
	the kernel owns pids, fds, wait and kill
	posix_spawn starts children declaratively
%size 4, center, fore "gold"

Many apps run concurrently - as processes.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

DO: provide a world programs share

	BlockFS makes files persistent across boots
	BusyBox hush is a real interactive shell
	pipes, ptys and job control compose programs
	/bin/cc builds new WebAssembly inside the OS
%size 4, center, fore "gold"

An environment is more than a runtime shim.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

DO: own the shared services

	a compositor combines process surfaces
	a client window manager supplies policy
	a sound server mixes every process into one output
	AF_UNIX gives system services local IPC
%size 4, center, fore "gold"

The browser supplies primitives, not the desktop.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

DO: define the failure boundaries

	separate instances provide address isolation
	file descriptors and resources have owners
	signals arrive cooperatively at safe points
	SIGKILL can still terminate a worker outright
%size 4, center, fore "gold"

The semantics are OS semantics, adapted honestly.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

DON'T: add threads by reflex

	WebAssembly threads require shared memory
	then come TLS, locks and thread-safe libc state
	processes already use multiple browser workers
	no current gucOS port requires pthreads
%size 4, center, fore "orange"

Parallelism does not have to mean pthreads.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

DON'T: pretend fork is cheap

%size 5, center, fore "white"
fork asks for a live process to become two.
%size 4, center, fore "gray70"
That means memory snapshots, stack tricks, and
reconciling resources that live outside memory.
%bar "orange" 12 15 70
%size 5, center, fore "SpringGreen"
gucOS chooses owner-brokered spawn instead.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

DON'T: promise preemption we lack

	no timer interrupt can seize arbitrary wasm code
	signals are observed at safe points
	blocking work yields through kernel operations
	pure compute can be killed, not gently interrupted
%size 4, center, fore "orange"

A precise limitation beats a fake abstraction.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

DON'T NEED TO: share the machine

	SMP is already below us
	the browser schedules workers across cores
	its sandbox isolates tabs from physical memory
	its security model mediates devices
%size 4, center, fore "DeepSkyBlue"

We schedule OS processes, not CPU cores.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

The browser is the HAL

%size 4, fore "gray70", center
HAL = hardware abstraction layer
%pause
%size 6, fore "white", left, prefix 20
display     WebGPU / canvas
audio       Web Audio
storage     OPFS
input       browser events
network     web transports
%pause
%size 5, fore "DeepSkyBlue", center, prefix " "

The web APIs are our device-driver boundary.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

DON'T NEED TO: reenact boot

	no BIOS, UEFI or bootloader
	no page tables or hardware MMU to program
	no ext4 layout tuned for a raw spinning disk
	no kernel driver for each physical device
%size 4, center, fore "DeepSkyBlue"

Those solved problems at a layer we do not own.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

Emulation takes the other bargain

	TinyEMU and v86 emulate a complete CPU
	a guest OS can preempt after virtual instructions
	it can boot Linux and keep its existing drivers
	but every guest instruction pays translation cost
%size 4, center, fore "orange"

Fidelity to another machine is the product there.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

This is a design space, not a race

	Browsix explored browser processes and fork
	WASIX extends the wasm system interface toward POSIX
	TinyEMU and v86 run full machines
	WebVM and CheerpX use JIT translation
%size 4, center, fore "gold"

Each project buys different semantics at a price.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

The gucOS rule

%size 6, center, fore "white"
Keep the contract when it helps programs.
%pause
%size 6, fore "orange"
Drop the mechanism when the substrate rejects it.
%pause
%size 6, fore "SpringGreen"
Build the missing system around the result.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

So what is the operating system?

%size 5, center, fore "gray70"
Not the first code to touch the hardware.
%size 6, center, fore "white"

It is the layer that turns available machinery
into a coherent world for programs and people.
%size 5, center, fore "DeepSkyBlue"

In gucOS, the browser is the HAL - not the OS.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

The source is part of the talk

	this deck is a plain-text .mgp file
	right-click it and choose Edit
	change the argument, the examples, the ending
	save; ctrl-r reloads it in the viewer
%size 5, center, fore "SpringGreen"

An operating system is editable infrastructure.
So is this presentation.
